(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-11-chance-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 11: Chance Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A randomized controlled trial of thrombolytic therapy versus angioplasty for acute myocardial infarction finds no difference in the main outcome, survival to discharge from hospital. The investigators explored whether this was also true for subgroups of patients defined by age, number of vessels affected, ejection fraction, comorbidity, and other patient characteristics. Which of the following is not true about this subgroup analysis?","choices":["Examining subgroups increases the chance of a false-positive (misleading statistically significant) result in one of the comparisons.","Examining subgroups increases the chance of a false-negative finding in one of these subgroups, relative to the main result.","Subgroup analyses are bad scientific practice and should not be done.","Reporting results in subgroups helps clinicians tailor information in the study to individual patients."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Although subgroup analyses risk false-positive and false-negative conclusions, they provide information that can help clinicians as long as their limitations are kept in mind. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.1"},{"q":"A new drug for hyperlipidemia was compared with placebo in a randomized controlled trial of 10,000 patients. After 2 years, serum cholesterol (the primary outcome) was 238 mg/dL in the group receiving the new drug and 240 mg/dL in the group receiving the old drug (P <0.001). Which of the following best describes the meaning of the P value in this study?","choices":["Bias is unlikely to account for the observed difference.","The difference is clinically important.","A difference as big as or bigger than what was observed could have arisen by chance less than one time in 1,000.","The results are generalizable to other patients with hypertension.","The statistical power of this study was inadequate."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The P value describes the risk of a false-positive conclusion and has nothing directly to do with bias and statistical power or the generalizability or clinical importance of the finding. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.2"},{"q":"In a well-designed clinical trial of treatment for ovarian cancer, remission rate at 1 year is 30% in patients offered a new drug and 20% in those offered a placebo. The P value is 0.4. Which of the following best describes the interpretation of this result?","choices":["Both treatments are effective.","Neither treatment is effective.","The statistical power of this study is 60%.","The best estimate of treatment effect size is 0.4.","There is insufficient information to decide whether one treatment is better than the other."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The P value is not small enough to establish that a treatment effect exists and provides no information on whether a clinically important effect could have been missed because of inadequate statistical power. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.3"},{"q":"In a cohort study, vitamin A intake was found to be a risk factor for hip fracture in women. The relative risk (highest quintile vs. lowest quintile) was 1.48, and the 95% confidence interval was 1.05–2.07. Which of the following best describes the meaning of this confidence interval?","choices":["The association is not statistically significant at the P <0.05 level.","A strong association between vitamin A intake and hip fracture was established.","The statistical power of this study is 95%.","There is a 95% chance that a range of relative risks as low as 1.05 and as high as 2.07 includes the true risk.","Bias is an unlikely explanation for this result."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The result was statistically significant, if one wanted to think of the role of chance in that way, because it excluded a relative risk of 1.0. Response D described the information the confidence interval contributes. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.4"},{"q":"Which of the following is the best reason for calling P ≤0.05 “statistically significant?”","choices":["It definitively rules out a false-positive conclusion.","It is an arbitrarily chosen but useful rule of thumb.","It rules out a type II error.","It is a way of establishing a clinically important effect size.","Larger or smaller P values do not provide useful information."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Calling P ≤0.05 “statistically significant” is a useful convention but otherwise has no particular mathematical or clinical meaning. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.5"},{"q":"Which of the following is the biggest advantage of multivariable modeling?","choices":["Models can control for many variables simultaneously.","Models do not depend on assumptions about the data.","There is a standardized and reproducible approach to modeling.","Models make stratified analyses unnecessary.","Models can control for confounding in large randomized controlled trials."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Models do depend on assumptions about the data, are not done in a standard way, and are meant to complement stratified analyses, not replace them. Although they might be used in large randomized trials, they are not particularly useful in that situation because randomization of a large number of patients has already made confounding very unlikely. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.6"},{"q":"A trial randomizes 30,000 patients to two treatment groups of similar size: one offered chemoprevention and the other usual care. How frequently must a side effect of chemoprevention occur for the study to have a good chance of observing at least one such side effect?","choices":["1/5,000","1/10,000","1/15,000","1/20,000","1/30,000"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Of the 30,000 people in the trial, about 15,000 would be in the chemoprevention arm of the trial. Applying the rule of thumb mentioned in this chapter (but solving for event rate, not sample size), 15,000 divided by 3 or a 1/5,000 event rate could be detected with 15,000 people under observation. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.7"},{"q":"A noninferiority randomized trial compared arthroscopic surgery versus physical therapy for knee injury with meniscal tears. The primary outcome was improvement in knee function, using a scale of 0 to 100, with better function indicated by higher scores. The noninferiority margin was defined as an 8-point difference in improvement, and a one-sided α level was set at 0.025. At 2 years follow-up, the knee surgery group had an improvement in function of 3.6 greater than the physical therapy group, P = 0.001 for noninferiority. Based on the results, which conclusion is true?","choices":["There was no clinically important difference between surgery and physical therapy.","There was a statistically significant greater improvement in knee function for the surgery group compared to the physical therapy group.","The study could not reject the null hypothesis based on these findings.","The study may have erroneously concluded a true difference exists between surgery and physical therapy.","A smaller margin would have been more likely to be statistically significant."],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The null hypothesis for this noninferiority trial is that the alternative treatment, physical therapy, is worse than the established treatment, surgery. The margin selected by the investigators is a judgment of what level of difference in function between the two groups would be clinically important. Although surgery had higher improvement in knee function scores than physical therapy, the difference between the two, 3.6, was below the margin of 8.0. In addition, significance testing showed that it would be unlikely that the true difference would be 8 or more. Thus the null hypothesis is rejected, and the conclusion from the study is that no clinically important difference exists between the two treatments, that is, physical therapy is noninferior to surgery. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.8"},{"q":"Which of the following is least related to the statistical power of a study with a dichotomous outcome?","choices":["Effect size","Type I error","Rate of outcome events in the control group","Type II error","The statistical test used"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Statistical power depends on the joint effects of all of the factors mentioned in A–D. It may vary a bit with the statistical test used to calculate it, but this is not the main determinate of sample size. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.9"},{"q":"Investigators derive a model to predict postoperative mortality from data on a cohort of patients undergoing surgery for hip fractures. The final model includes variables for heart function (ejection fraction), kidney function (creatinine), and age. Asthma was assessed for inclusion in the model, but did not affect the prediction. The model was found to have excellent discrimination (sensitivity = 0.91, specificity = 0.93, c-statistic = 0.88) and internal calibration. Which of the following is true?","choices":["The positive predictive values can be calculated from the provided performance characteristics.","The model performance indicates that it would be useful for risk stratification.","Asthma is a confounder and thus excluded from the final model.","Age confounds the other model variables and could be removed from the prediction model.","Age, creatinine, and ejection fraction are risk factors for postoperative mortality in this study."],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Prediction models are derived from multivariable methods to identify a set of variables that each improves the prediction of the outcome of interest. The final model shows how risk factors can be used together to improve prediction. In single risk factor studies, the model is used to control for other confounders to determine the independent effect of the variable of primary interest. Even models that perform well in the derivation population need to be validated independently in different populations. In addition, studies assessing the impact of the model on clinical outcomes also are an important part of model validation. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.10"},{"q":"Which of the following best characterizes the application of Bayesian reasoning to a clinical trial?","choices":["Prior belief in the comparative effectiveness of treatment is guided by equipoise.","The results of each new study change belief in treatment effect from what it was before the study.","Bayesian inference is an alternative way of calculating a P value.","Bayesian reasoning is based, like inferential statistics, on the null hypothesis.","Bayesian reasoning depends on a well-defined hypothesis before the study is begun."],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bayesian reasoning is about how new information affects prior belief and has nothing to do with inferential statistics or the ethical rationale for randomized trials. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.11"},{"q":"In a randomized trial of intensive glucose lowering in type 2 diabetes, death rate was higher in the intensively treated patients: hazard ratio 1.22 (95% confidence interval 1.01–1.46). Which of the following is not true about this study?","choices":["The results are consistent with almost no effect.","The best estimate of treatment effect is a hazard ratio of 1.22.","If a P value were calculated, the results would be statistically significant at the 0.05 level.","A P value would provide as much information as the confidence interval.","The results are consistent with 46% higher death rates in the intensively treated patients."],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The results are consistent with a 1% up to a 46% higher death rate, with the best estimate being 22% higher, and excludes a hazard ratio of 1.0 (no effect), so it is “statistically significant.” Confidence intervals provide more information than a P value for the same data because they include the point estimate and range of values that is likely to include the true effect. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 11.12"}]};
